/* V90 — the retry, and the reason to press it.

   ---- What the measurement said, and what it did not ----

   The plan for this phase was "death to the next run in under 400 milliseconds", on the assumption
   that the loop was slow. It is not. Measured on a 390x844 phone, over three consecutive solo runs,
   pressing the primary on the first frame the results screen exists:

     finish() itself      6-12ms
     -> screen on glass   20-29ms
     press -> begin()     8-14ms
     -> first frame       23-31ms
     TOTAL                44-59ms

   Forty-five milliseconds. The machine was never the problem, and building a fast path would have
   been a week spent optimising something already twenty times inside its budget. That is the whole
   value of measuring before building.

   The same measurement found the real fault by accident. The primary button's rect across those
   three runs, same mode, same phone, nothing else changed:

     run 1   y = 484
     run 2   y = 509
     run 3   y = 484

   **The button moves.** It sits in a flex row at the bottom of a panel whose height depends on how
   many lines V37's verdict and read happen to wrap to, whether V45 has a board line, whether V58
   has a challenge line, and whether NEXT STAGE or REMATCH is in the row beside it. So the thumb
   cannot learn where it is. Twenty-five pixels is not much to look at and it is everything to a
   thumb travelling blind: you arrive, the button is not there, and you have to read the screen
   again. That is the cost of the retry loop in this game, and it is measured in the time it takes a
   person to re-find a target, not in milliseconds of script.

   ---- Part one: the button stops moving ----

   One bar, pinned to the bottom of the viewport, the same rect on every results screen in every
   mode. It is at the bottom because that is where the thumb already is: the reactor, the pilot,
   V83's magazine and V76's wells all live in the bottom third of the arena, so the hand does not
   travel at all between the last tap of one run and the first press of the next.

   It does not replace the logic underneath it. The bar forwards its press to whichever real button
   the existing layers decided should be primary — NEXT STAGE if V14 unhid it, REMATCH if V29 did,
   PLAY AGAIN otherwise — so campaign progression, duel rematch and the daily's seed handling all
   keep working through exactly the code that already handles them. The row's own copy of that
   button is hidden, because two primaries is worse than a moving one.

   ---- Part two: a field worth learning ----

   A button that repeats is only worth pressing if the thing it repeats is worth repeating, and
   until now solo has been a fresh random arena every time. That is why the results screen has to be
   read: the run that just ended taught you nothing about the next one, so the only thing left to do
   with it is look at the number.

   So solo now runs on **one fixed field until you beat your own score on it.** The first run on a
   field sets the mark. Every run after it is the same arrivals in the same places at the same
   times, and beating the mark retires the field and deals a new one.

   That turns a death into information. You know what is coming at wave four because you have seen
   wave four; a mistake becomes something you can specifically not make again; and the target is a
   number you have personally already proved is reachable, because you are the one who set it. It
   also gives the bar something to say other than "again", which is the difference between a button
   and a reason.

   The machinery is V39's, unchanged and already verified deterministic: the same seed produced an
   identical sequence of 19 spawns — type, position, speed, radius and elite modifier — while a
   different seed diverged, so the check can fail. Nothing here re-implements any of it.

   The one thing designed against is being stuck. A mark you cannot beat is a field that has stopped
   teaching, so after enough attempts the results screen quietly offers a fresh one. It is a
   secondary in the row rather than a second pinned button: the pinned position has one meaning. */

const V90={
 on:true,
 /* Attempts on one field before the escape hatch appears. Six is roughly four minutes of play — long
    enough that leaving early is quitting on it, short enough that a field you have genuinely stopped
    improving on does not become the whole game. */
 stale:6
};

/* ---- The field ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Kept on `save` so it rides the existing persist rather than owning a second storage key, and
   normalised on every read because a save written by an older build has none of these. */

function v90Field(){
 if(!save.v90||typeof save.v90!=='object')save.v90={seed:0,best:0,tries:0};
 const f=save.v90;
 if(!Number.isFinite(f.seed)||f.seed<=0)f.seed=v90NewSeed();
 if(!Number.isFinite(f.best))f.best=0;
 if(!Number.isFinite(f.tries))f.tries=0;
 return f;
}

function v90NewSeed(){
 /* crypto rather than Math.random for the same reason `begin` uses it: this is the identity of a
    field somebody may spend twenty minutes on, and two players landing on the same one by accident
    would make the "your field" framing a lie. */
 try{return crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint32Array(1))[0]||1;}
 catch(e){return (Math.floor(Math.random()*4294967295)||1);}
}

function v90Deal(){
 const f=v90Field();
 f.seed=v90NewSeed();
 f.best=0;
 f.tries=0;
 persist();
 return f;
}

/* Solo only, and only when nobody upstream supplied a seed. The three callers that do supply one —
   V39's daily, V58's challenge link, and the duel — are each playing somebody else's field on
   purpose, and this must never overwrite that. Outermost, so `mode` is still the caller's word:
   V39's own wrapper rewrites 'daily' to 'solo' further down, and by then the distinction is gone. */
const v90Begin=begin;
begin=function(mode='solo',seed){
 const own=V90.on&&mode==='solo'&&seed===undefined;
 const out=v90Begin(mode,own?v90Field().seed:seed);
 game.v90Field=own;
 /* Cleared per run, not per finish: it is the reason the last results screen was celebrating, and a
    celebration that survives into the next run's results screen is a lie about that run. */
 game.v90Beaten=0;
 return out;
};

const v90Finish=finish;
finish=function(victory=false,duelData=null){
 const own=game.running&&game.v90Field;
 const score=Math.floor(game.score);
 const f=own?v90Field():null;
 /* Read before the base runs, because the mark that matters is the one the player was playing
    against — not the one their own run is about to become. */
 const mark=f?f.best:0;
 v90Finish(victory,duelData);
 if(!f)return;

 f.tries++;
 /* A mark of zero is an unset mark, not a mark of zero. The run that sets it cannot also beat it —
    otherwise a fresh field is retired by any score at all and solo is random again with extra
    steps — and a run that scores nothing leaves the field still waiting for its first mark rather
    than handing the next run a target it clears by existing. */
 const beat=mark>0&&score>mark;
 if(score>f.best)f.best=score;
 if(beat){
  v90Deal();
  game.v90Beaten=mark;
 }else{
  persist();
 }
 v90Bar();
};

/* ---- The bar -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Built here rather than in index.html because it has to exist for every screen state without any
   layer having to remember to show it: its visibility is one CSS rule keyed on the screen V13's
   showScreen already stamps onto the body. Nothing has to call anything to make it appear. */

let v90El=null;
let v90Label=null;
let v90Note=null;
let v90Fresh=null;

function v90Build(){
 if(v90El||!V90.on)return;
 v90El=document.createElement('div');
 v90El.id='v90Bar';
 v90El.innerHTML='<button id="v90Again" class="primary"><b></b><small></small></button>';
 document.body.appendChild(v90El);
 v90Label=v90El.querySelector('b');
 v90Note=v90El.querySelector('small');
 /* Forwarded rather than reimplemented. Every rule about what "again" means in this mode — the
    campaign's next stage, the duel's rematch, the daily starting the same field rather than a
    random one — already lives on the button being clicked. */
 v90El.querySelector('button').addEventListener('click',()=>{
  const t=v90Target();
  if(t)t.click();
 });

 /* The label has to follow the buttons it mirrors, and one of them moves on its own clock: V29 holds
    a duel's results screen open while the rival is still playing and only unhides REMATCH when the
    server reports the final standings, minutes after any finish or screen change this part sees.

    The forwarded action was never wrong — `v90Target` re-reads the classes at click time — but a bar
    reading AGAIN that starts a rematch is worse than one that reads wrong and does nothing. */
 const watch=new MutationObserver(()=>v90Bar());
 for(const id of ['nextStageBtn','rematchBtn','againBtn']){
  const el=$(id);
  if(el)watch.observe(el,{attributes:true,attributeFilter:['class']});
 }
}

/* Reads the `hidden` class rather than computed display, because the row's own primaries are hidden
   by a stylesheet from here on and every other layer still toggles the class. */
function v90Visible(id){
 const el=$(id);
 return el&&!el.classList.contains('hidden');
}

function v90Target(){
 if(v90Visible('nextStageBtn'))return $('nextStageBtn');
 if(v90Visible('rematchBtn'))return $('rematchBtn');
 return $('againBtn');
}

function v90Bar(){
 if(!V90.on)return;
 v90Build();
 if(!v90El)return;
 const t=v90Target();
 const id=t?t.id:'againBtn';
 const f=v90Field();

 if(id==='nextStageBtn'){
  v90Label.textContent='NEXT STAGE';
  v90Note.textContent='';
 }else if(id==='rematchBtn'){
  v90Label.textContent='REMATCH';
  v90Note.textContent='BOTH OF YOU HAVE TO TAP IT';
 }else{
  v90Label.textContent='AGAIN';
  v90Note.textContent=v90Caption(f);
 }
 v90El.classList.toggle('v90-beaten',Boolean(game.v90Beaten));
 v90Stale(f);
}

function v90Caption(f){
 /* The daily, a challenge link and the duel are somebody else's field, and the campaign has its own
    ladder. Saying "same field" on any of them would be false. */
 if(game.mode!=='solo')return '';
 if(game.v90Beaten)return 'FIELD BEATEN — A NEW ONE IS DEALT';
 if(f.tries<=1&&f.best>0)return `NEW FIELD — THE MARK IS ${format(f.best)}`;
 if(f.best>0)return `SAME FIELD — BEAT ${format(f.best)}`;
 return 'A NEW FIELD — SET THE MARK';
}

/* The escape hatch. A secondary in the row, appearing only once the field has stopped teaching, and
   never in the pinned position — that position means one thing. */
function v90Stale(f){
 const row=$('resultScreen')&&$('resultScreen').querySelector('.button-row');
 if(!row)return;
 if(!v90Fresh){
  v90Fresh=document.createElement('button');
  v90Fresh.id='v90FreshBtn';
  v90Fresh.className='secondary';
  v90Fresh.textContent='DEAL A NEW FIELD';
  v90Fresh.addEventListener('click',()=>{
   v90Deal();
   toast('NEW FIELD DEALT');
   v90Bar();
  });
  row.insertBefore(v90Fresh,row.firstChild);
 }
 const show=V90.on&&game.mode==='solo'&&!game.v90Beaten&&f.tries>=V90.stale;
 v90Fresh.classList.toggle('hidden',!show);
}

/* Repainted on every screen change rather than only on finish: V29 fills the duel's result in later,
   when the server reports how the match actually ended, and V14 unhides NEXT STAGE inside its own
   finish wrapper. Both land after the base finish this part wraps. */
const v90ShowScreen=showScreen;
showScreen=function(id){
 const out=v90ShowScreen(id);
 if(V90.on&&id==='resultScreen')v90Bar();
 return out;
};
